    "probe_text": "## 1. Concepts and rules, in dependency order\n\n**Raw numbers** (`Person.java`, `V1__people.sql`)\n- `alpha`, `beta`, `gamma`: optional whole numbers (NULL means blank). A blank counts as zero in every calculation (`orZero`).\n- `hold`: a true/false flag.\n- `patrons`: links to other people (`person_patron`).\n\n**Per-person values** (`PersonView.java`; the server is the only place they are calculated)\n1. `thrust` = alpha \u00d7 beta\n2. `momentum` = thrust + 3 \u00d7 (gamma \u2212 alpha)\n3. `scaled` = momentum \u00d7 2\n4. `third` = scaled \u00f7 3, rounded\n5. `quarter` = scaled \u00f7 4, rounded\n6. `tier` = `SINK` if scaled < 0, `STEADY` for 0\u2013199, `SURGE` for 200 or more\n7. `averageScaled` (one value for the whole sheet): the mean of scaled over people not on hold whose scaled isn't n/a, rounded. It is null if nobody qualifies.\n8. `norm` = scaled \u2212 averageScaled\n9. **Patron aggregate:**\n   - `mutualPatrons`: patronage goes both ways. Each person's patrons are the people they name plus the people who name them. Each counts once, and naming yourself is ignored.\n   - `backing` = the sum of those patrons' momentum, skipping n/a ones. It is n/a if the person's own momentum is.\n10. **`keel`** = 2\u00b7scaled + 2\u00b7momentum + 2\u00b7norm + 2\u00b7thrust. It is n/a when momentum or norm is.\n\n**Computed from keel** (each is n/a when keel is n/a, or when any other input it uses is n/a)\n- **Chained:**\n  - `fathom` = keel + momentum + 68\n  - `cascade` = fathom + scaled + 72\n  - `ripple` = cascade + keel + 76\n  - `ballast` = ripple + thrust + 80\n  - `keystone` = ballast + norm + 84\n- **Keel plus a base value plus a constant:**\n  - `draft` = keel + thrust + 88\n  - `trim` = keel \u2212 thrust + 92\n  - `heading` = keel + norm + 96\n  - `bearing` = keel \u2212 norm + 104\n  - `pitch` = keel + momentum + 108\n  - `tackle` = keel \u2212 momentum + 112\n  - `yaw` = keel + scaled + 116\n  - `heave` = keel \u2212 scaled + 120\n- **Keel and a constant:**\n  - `camber` = keel + 200 + 124\n  - `sheer` = keel \u2212 100 + 128\n  - `beam` = keel\u00d73 + 132\n  - `buoyancy` = keel\u00d73 + 140\n  - `freeboard` = keel\u00f73 + 136\n  - `knot` = keel\u00f73 + 144\n  - `mast` = keel + 2000\n  - `pier` = keel + 500\n  - `ebb` = keel \u2212 500\n  - `wharf` = keel\u00d75\n  - `rigging` = keel\u00f74\n  - `jetty` = keel\u00f710\n- **Keel paired with another value:**\n  - `cable` = keel + gamma\n  - `hull` = keel \u2212 gamma\n  - `bow` = keel + fathom\n  - `stern` = keel \u2212 fathom\n  - `helm` = keel + ripple\n  - `rudder` = keel \u2212 ripple\n  - `moor` = keel + keystone\n  - `berth` = keel \u2212 keystone\n  - `flood` = keel + ballast\n  - `swell` = keel \u2212 ballast\n\n**Sheet-wide rules**\n- **Rounding:** every division goes through `divide` (`SheetMath.divide` on the server, and a copy of it in `people-list.ts`). It rounds to the nearest whole number, with halves rounding up (5/2 \u2192 3, \u22125/2 \u2192 \u22122).\n- **Holds:**\n  - People on hold are left out of `averageScaled`.\n  - They are left out of every `\u2026Low`, `\u2026Top`, `\u2026Spread` and `\u2026Mean` rollup.\n  - In totals they add half their value, rounded.\n  - They are never counted.\n- **Trimming:** `trimmedTotal` (front end only) applies to every `\u2026Total` (thrust, keel, fathom, \u2026 camber). It drops n/a values, halves the values of people on hold, then removes one highest and one lowest value whenever at least two remain.\n- **Which people count:** `countable` (front end only) drops people on hold and anyone with keel < 0. It is used by `keelCount` and every `\u2026Even` count (how many people have an even value).\n- **Rollups:** `\u2026Low`/`\u2026Top` are the min/max over people not on hold, `\u2026Spread` = top \u2212 low, and `\u2026Mean` = the rounded average over people not on hold.\n\n## 2. How hard it was to follow\n\nAll the per-person maths is in one place, `PersonView.java`, but it's one long chain of nearly identical methods and a single wall-of-text Javadoc. The sheet-wide rules (trimming, counting, holds in rollups) live separately, at the bottom of a 1,163-line Angular component, next to around 80 copy-pasted `computed` blocks. And the comments disagree with the code: the front end's `Person` interface comments leave out many of the constants and say `beam` is keel \u00d7 2 (the server uses keel \u00d7 3 + 132), and `keelCount` actually counts even keels. A new developer could find the logic, but would have to work through it carefully, and couldn't trust the comments."
